.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 16:30:16  dlr
.\" Initial revision: mdsplit(1) man page. All 13 options including -d, -e and -S which the built-in usage omits. Documents the basename/type filename convention and the prefix, infix and suffix modifiers.
.\"
.TH MDSPLIT 1 "2026-08-29" "mdsplit 1.30" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
mdsplit \- split hash lists into solved and unsolved files by hash type
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdsplit
.RI [ options ]
.IR hashfile " [" hashfile " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdsplit
reads
.BR mdxfind (1)
result lines from standard input, or from the file named by
.BR \-f ,
and uses them to partition the named
.I hashfile
arguments.  Each hash that appears in the results is removed from its
unsolved file and written to a per-type solution file; each hash that does
not is left behind.  The unsolved list shrinks as the campaign progresses.
.PP
The naming convention it maintains is:
.PP
.RS
.TP 16
.IB name .txt
the unsolved hashes
.TP
.IB name .MD5x01
solved hashes of that type and iteration depth
.TP
.IB name .SHA1x02
and so on, one file per distinct
.IB TYPE x NN
seen
.RE
.PP
Only
.B .txt
files are examined by default.  Use
.B \-a
to process every file whose basename matches.
.PP
A result line is
.IB TYPE x NN
.IB hash : password
or
.IB TYPE x NN
.IB hash : salt : password\fR.
Lines with no type label are attributed to the type given by
.BR \-t .
.PP
.B mdsplit
matches on the hash and treats everything after it as opaque, so a password
that
.BR mdxfind (1)
wrapped in
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
is filed through unchanged and unexamined.  Given the result lines
.PP
.RS
.nf
MD5x01 48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]
MD5x01 07117fe4a1ebd544965dc19573183da2:$HEX[636166c3a9]
.fi
.RE
.PP
the two hashes leave
.B list.txt
and arrive in
.B list.MD5x01
with their wrappers intact:
.PP
.RS
.nf
48c83cd71d44bbe192c77258b567c417:$HEX[706173733a776f7264]
07117fe4a1ebd544965dc19573183da2:$HEX[636166c3a9]
.fi
.RE
.PP
This matters because a password containing a colon would otherwise make the
solution file ambiguous.  Use
.BR getpass (1)
.B \-u
to recover the plaintext, or
.BR rehex (1)
.B \-u
to decode the wrapper directly.
Hashes are matched by longest prefix, so truncated hashes still resolve, and
by default reversed hashes are also recognised.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read result lines from
.I file
instead of standard input.
.TP
.BI \-t " TYPExNN"
Assume this type for result lines that carry no type label, e.g.
.BR MD5x01 .
Between 3 and 64 characters.
.TP
.B \-a
Process all files matching the basename, not just
.BR .txt .
.TP
.B \-e
Require an exact match.  Disables the longest-prefix matching used for
truncated hashes.
.TP
.B \-r
Do not scan for reversed hashes.  The default is to try both orientations.
.TP
.BI \-S " char"
Field separator character.  Default
.BR : .
.TP
.BI \-p " string"
Prepend
.I string
to each solution filename.  When a prefix is given the directory part of
the input basename is stripped, so solutions can be collected somewhere
other than beside their source.
.TP
.BI \-i " string"
Insert
.I string
between the basename and the type in each solution filename.
.TP
.BI \-s " string"
Append
.I string
to each solution filename.
.TP
.BI \-b " size"
Buffer size, with an optional
.BR B ,
.BR K ,
.B M
or
.B G
suffix.  Bare numbers are kilobytes.  Not supported on Windows.
.TP
.B \-l
Do not lock files.  Use on filesystems that cannot lock, such as some
network mounts.  Concurrent
.B mdsplit
runs against the same files then become unsafe.
.TP
.B \-d
Print detailed per-type statistics at the end of the run.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-?
Print the usage summary and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on normal completion.  Non-zero on a usage error or an unreadable input.
.SH EXAMPLES
The common pipeline \(em search, then split the results back over the
unsolved lists in one pass:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cat *.txt | mdxfind \-i 3 /tmp/words | mdsplit *.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Apply results collected earlier to a different set of unsolved lists:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cat foo.res bar.res | mdsplit /other/unsolved
.fi
.RE
.PP
Split from a saved result file, labelling untyped lines as MD5x01:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mdsplit \-f foo.res \-t MD5x01 biglist.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR getpass (1),
.BR hashpipe (1),
.BR rling (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
